From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joel Winarske <joel.winarske@linux.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 14:24:53 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] Add a Sequoia backend for OpenPGP signature verification

libostree verifies OpenPGP signatures through GPGME, which runs gpg. That
puts GnuPG, which is GPL-3.0-only, on every device that pulls from a
signed remote.

--with-sequoia builds the same GPG code paths on openpgp-verify-sequoia,
a small C interface over the Sequoia OpenPGP library (LGPL-2.0-or-later),
in place of GPGME. Covered are what a device needs: checking detached
signatures against the keyrings of a remote, with the results that the
OstreeGpgVerifyResult API reports, and importing keys into the keyring
of a remote. Signing, listing keys and importing from the keyring of
the user are not, and fail saying so: they are done where gpg is.

A signature is valid only while its key and certificate are neither
expired nor revoked, as gpg has it. Sequoia's standard policy applies,
which unlike gpg rejects SHA-1 data signatures.

test-gpg-verify-result reads the GPGME result and is not built.

Upstream-Status: Pending [prototype for https://github.com/ostreedev/ostree/issues/2304]
Signed-off-by: Joel Winarske <joel.winarske@linux.com>
